# Slider-crank driven through a rigid rod (distance loop joint): crank
# (revolute z) and slider (prismatic x) are separate branches off the base,
# tied by a rod of length 1.0 between a pin on the crank and a pin on the
# slider -- the classic mechanism, connecting rod modeled as the massless
# distance constraint. Out-of-plane pin offsets and tilted gravity keep the
# geometry generic so no symbolic term dies to planar sparsity.
#
# nc = 1 = rank(K), mobility = 2 tree DOF - 1 = 1. Also the only model whose
# two loop paths meet at the BASE with both paths nonempty (fourbar and rrrs
# close a body back to the base), exercising that side of the K path walk.
#
# Rod length 1.0 comfortably exceeds the worst-case transverse gap
# (~0.36 m), so the crank turns fully and a slider position exists at every
# crank angle.

gravity 0.5 -2.0 -9.81
stab 0.05

body                     # 0: crank, spins about base z
parent -1
joint revolute z
tree 0 0 0
mass 1.2
cg 0.15 0 0.05
inertia 0.02 0.03 0.03 0.005 0 0
fext 1

body                     # 1: slider, slides along base x, raised bed
parent -1
joint prismatic x
tree 0.9 0 0.25
mass 0.8
cg 0.02 0 0
inertia 0.01 0.015 0.015 0 0 0
fext 1

loop                     # rod: crank pin to slider pin, both off the plane
predecessor 0
successor 1
joint distance
ptree 0.3 0 0.1
stree 0 0.05 -0.1
length 1.0
